:203998472 a :Record ;
	:title "Unraveling plant specialised metabolism leveraging retrobiosynthesis tools and genome-scalem metabolic models" ;
	:type "masterThesis" ;
	:description "Plant metabolism can be divided into two different types: primary and secondary metabolism. Primary\nmetabolism supports fundamental growth and reproduction processes, remaining highly conserved across\nplant species. In contrast, secondary metabolism fosters the production of specialized compounds like\nalkaloids, serving specific roles in plant-environment interactions, which vary considerably between and\nwithin plant species. Due to its intricacy, many pathways associated with these compounds remain insufficiently\ncharacterized. Some bioinformatics techniques offer essential insights into unveiling specialized\nplant metabolism pathways, like retrobiosynthesis, inspired by the retro-evolution hypothesis. This computational\napproach aims to predict new routes by breaking down the structure of a target molecule into a\nset of increasingly simple structures. It can be performed by template-based or template-free tools, using\nreaction rules, Simplified Molecular-Input Line-Entry System (SMILES), and/or SMILES Arbitrary Target\nSpecification (SMARTS), which are computational representations for compounds and reactions, and/or\ntransformer models. In this work, a benchmark was conducted on retrosynthesis tools using an independent\ndataset comprising several secondary metabolite case studies. Among the tools evaluated, the\nREADRetro tool demonstrated the best performance, achieving higher prediction accuracy due to enhancements\nmade through transfer learning with a plant-specific dataset. A single-step retrosynthesis evaluation\nof various transfer learning approaches identified the optimal model, which was subsequently applied to\npredict pathways for alkaloids with unresolved biosynthetic routes. The model successfully proposed pathways\nfor three alkaloids; however, these pathways did not meet the necessary criteria for incorporation\ninto Genome-Scale Metabolic Models (GSMMs), and thus, the metabolic validity of these routes could not\nbe assessed." ;
	:language "eng" ;
	:rights "openAccess" ;
	:createdBy :Luciana_Costa_Martins ;
	:contributionBy :Miguel_Rocha, :Oscar_Dias ;
	:hasSubject :Secondary_Metabolites, :Genome_Scale_Metabolic_Models, :Deep_Learning, :Retrobiosynthesis, :Metabolitos_Secundários, :Modelos_Metabólicos_à_Escala_do_Genoma, :Retrobiossíntese ;
