	:hasThumbnail :Joel_Tomas_Morais_pdf_jpg .

:202481123 a :Record ;
	:title "The influence of emotions on human computer interaction" ;
	:type "masterThesis" ;
	:description "Emotion is an essential part of what means to be human, but it is still disregarded by\nmost technical fields as something not to be taken into account in scientific or engineering\nprojects. However, the understanding of emotion as an aspect of decision making processes\nand of modelling of human behaviour is essential in order to create a better connection between\nhumans and their tools and machines. This dissertation focuses on the measurement\nof emotion of users through the use of non-intrusive methods, like measuring inputs and\nreactions to stimuli, along with the creation of a tool that measures the emotional changes\ncaused by visual output created by the tool itself. Usage of the tool in a test environment\nand the subsequent analysis of the data obtained will allow for conclusions about the effectiveness\nof the method, and if it is possible to apply it to future studies on human emotions\nby investigators in the fields of psychology and computation.", "Emoção é uma parte essencial do que nos faz humanos, mas mesmo assim há muitos\nnos campos mais técnicos e científicos que a desconsideram como algo que não deve ser\ntido em conta em projetos científicos ou de engenharia. A compreensão da emoção é no\nentanto um aspeto chave para o estudo dos processos de decisão humanos, assim como\npara a modelação de comportamentos, algo essencial para a criação de uma melhor ligação\nentre o homem e as suas máquinas e ferramentas. Esta dissertação foca-se na medição\nda emoção de utilizadores através de métodos não-intrusivos, como medições de inputs\nem computadores e reações a estímulos, assim como na criação de uma ferramenta que\nmeça as mudanças emocionais causadas nos utilizadores por certos estímulos visuais provenientes\nda própria ferramenta. Utilização da ferramenta num ambiente de teste e a subsequente\nanálise dos dados obtidos permitirão a tomada de conclusões sobre a eficiência deste\nmétodo, assim como sobre se é viável a aplicação do mesmo a estudos futuros da emoção\nhumana realizados por investigadores nas áreas de psicologia ou computação." ;
	:language "eng" ;
	:rights "openAccess" ;
	:createdBy :André_Francisco_Soares_Carvalho_Alves_Teixeira ;
	:contributionBy :Paulo_Novais, :Davide_Rua_Carneiro ;
	:issuedDate "2018-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:availableDate "2020-05-12T14:07:06Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:accessionedDate "2020-05-12T14:07:06Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:submittedDate "2018-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:grade "13 valores" ;
	:hasOriginal :André_Francisco_Soares_Carvalho_Alves_Teixeira_dissertação_pdf ;
	:hasThumbnail :André_Francisco_Soares_Carvalho_Alves_Teixeira_dissertação_pdf_jpg .

:101698062 a :Record ;
	:title "The interplay between quantum foundations and circuit complexity" ;
	:type "doctoralThesis" ;
	:description "Quantum computers were initially proposed to surpass the limitations of classical computers in simulating physical systems. Soon after, the field of quantum computing advanced through landmark discoveries of quantum algorithms, with some conjectured to provide exponential advantages over classical counterparts. These include Shor’s factoring algorithm, which has profound implications in cryptography. Additionally, moderate yet significant polynomial separations have further increased interest in the field. However, despite this progress, unconditional proofs confirming quantum superiority on the theoretical side remain elusive. At the same time, large, noise-resilient quantum computations on the experimental side, which could challenge the extended Church-Turing thesis by potentially breaking classical computational barriers, have yet to be achieved. Meanwhile, our understanding of the differences between classical and quantum mechanics has deepened. We now possess rigorous theoretical tools, such as Bell inequalities and nonlocal games, to describe behaviors unique to these theories. Moreover, the main loopholes for observing non-classical correlations have been closed experimentally, showing that correlations in the physical world exceed those predicted by classical mechanics. This thesis investigates computational complexity differences between quantum and classical models by studying shallow-depth circuits. The primary focus lies in identifying, elucidating, and proving computational quantum advantages. Additionally, the analysis delves into the bijection between these circuits and foundational non-local game constructions, exploring how quantum-classical deviations in multi-party correlation settings translate to computational advantages. A key question addressed is how to synthesize constant-depth quantum circuits for various problems to leverage quantum advantages. To this end, circuit synthesis techniques tailored to measurement-based quantum computation are developed, enabling simultaneously optimal quantum strategies for certain nonlocal games based on a particular bijection between this model and XOR non-local games. Building on this work, we confront a pivotal challenge: If a candidate quantum circuit potentially provides a computational advantage, how can we prove that no classical circuit surpasses it? This work presents new unconditional quantum-classical separations within shallow-depth settings, distinguishing for the first time constant-depth quantum circuits with one- and two-qubit gates from biased polynomial threshold circuits, which incorporate neural-network-type functionalities. These separations extend to prime-dimensional quantum systems, and we conjecture to have identified Razborov-Smolensky-type problems uniquely solvable by specific prime dimensions. The thesis also differentiates constant-depth quantum circuits with multi-qubit Toffoli gates from their classical counterparts, emphasizing the power of parallel quantum computation. Finally, verifying shallow-depth quantum computations is challenging due to conjectured exponential separations from classical computations, limiting correctness verification through simulation. While specific quantum algorithms within BQP have been addressed, we explore whether a quantum computer can reliably execute any instance from a fixed class of quantum circuits. Moving beyond heuristic-based solutions, we propose a benchmarking protocol that certifies, with exponentially high confidence, the execution of arbitrary circuits of specified dimensions, widths, and depths." ;
	:language "eng" ;
	:rights "openAccess" ;
	:createdBy :Michael_Oliveira ;
	:contributionBy :Ernesto_Fagundes_Galvão, :Elham_Kashefi, :L_S_Barbosa ;
	:hasSubject :Non_local_Games, :Quantum_Benchmarks, :Quantum_Circuit_Complexity, :Benchmarks_Quânticos, :Complexidade_de_Circuitos_Quânticos, :Jogos_Não_locais ;
	:issuedDate "2025-12-03T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:accessionedDate "2026-01-19T16:25:07Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:submittedDate "2025-02-01T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:grade "Muito bom" ;
