:203017960 a :Record ;
	:title "Deep learning applied to medical imaging" ;
	:type "masterThesis" ;
	:description "Medical examinations in general and medical imaging in particular play an important role in disease \ndetection and patient monitoring. Most of the medical images produced are based on X-rays and are used \nby almost all medical specialties. They provide sensitive results and are relatively inexpensive compared \nto other techniques such as Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI). However, MRI has proven to be a great \ndiagnostic tool as it enables high-quality images in multiple planes or directions.\nProgressively more researchers are exploring the use of Deep Learning (DL) algorithms to process and \nanalyse medical images. The main goal of this work is to study and evaluate DL methods applied to two \ndifferent types of medical images: 2D X-rays images and 3D MRI scans. Both of these types are often \nused for early detection, diagnosis, and treatment of diseases. Existing DL architectures and image pre processing techniques have also been studied and developed for segmentation of various brain regions \n(using MRIs) and classification of chest diseases (using X-rays).\nConvolution Neural Network (CNN) architectures have aroused interest in health domain because of their \npowerful learning ability. These networks use multiple convolutions, pooling and fully connected layers to \nlearn the features. This work investigated several CNN architectures and their application in classification \nof chest X-rays. The use of transfer learning, a deep learning technique, was also studied. This technique \nenabled the initialization of the CNN with weights from a pre-trained model, whereby the knowledge was \ntransferred from another model. Fully Convolutional Networks (FCN), have been successfully used in the \nsegmentation of biomedical images. In this work, an FCN based on the well-known U-net architecture was \nused to perform the segmentation of brain MRI scans. U-net uses convolution, max pooling, concatenation \nand up sampling layers to extract the features. For the segmentation task the best result obtained with \nbrain MRI using Dice Similarity Coefficient was 95.97%. For the classification task using chest X-rays, the \nbest result using Area Under the ROC Curve was 96.40%.", "Os exames médicos em geral e as imagens médicas em particular, desempenham um papel importante \nna deteção de doenças e no monitoramento dos pacientes. As imagens produzidas são na sua maioria\nraios-X, pois são utilizados em grande parte das especialidades médicas. Os raios-X fornecem resultados \nsensíveis e são relativamente baratos quando comparados com outras técnicas, como a ressonância \nmagnética (RM). As RM, no entanto, provaram ser uma ótima ferramenta de diagnóstico, uma vez que\npermitem obter imagens de alta qualidade em vários planos ou direções.\nProgressivamente mais cientistas investigam o uso de algoritmos de Deep Learning (DL) para o\nprocessamento e análise de imagens médicas. O presente trabalho tem como principal objetivo o estudo\ne avaliação de técnicas de DL aplicadas a dois tipos de imagens médicas: imagens de raios-X (2D) e \nimagens de RM (3D). Ambas são frequentemente usados para deteção precoce, diagnóstico e tratamento \nde doenças. Estudaram-se também diferentes arquiteturas de DL e técnicas de pré-processamento de \nimagens para realizar a segmentação de várias regiões do cérebro (usando imagens de RM) e para a \nclassificação de doenças torácicas (usando raios-X).\nA Convolution Neural Network (CNN) é uma arquitetura que tem despertado um grande interesse no \ndomínio da saúde devido à sua boa capacidade de aprendizagem. Estas redes utilizam várias camadas\nde tipo convolution, pooling e fully connected para aprender as features. O presente trabalho investigou \ndiferentes esquemas de CNN e avaliou a sua capacidade de classificar os raios-X tóracicos. Também se \nestudou o uso de uma técnica de DL denominada transfer learning. Esta técnica permitiu a inicialização \nda rede usando pesos provenientes de um modelo pré-treinado, transferindo o seu conhecimento. As\nFully Convolutional Networks (FCN) têm obtido um bom desempenho na segmentação de imagens \nbiomédicas. Neste trabalho, para segmentar as imagens de RM do cérebro, utilizou-se uma FCN baseada \nnuma arquitetura amplamente conhecida, a arquitetura U-Net. A U-Net usa camadas de convolution, max\npooling, concatenation e up sampling para extrair as features.\nNa segmentação de imagens de RM cerebral, o melhor resultado foi um Coeficiente de Similaridade dos \nDados de 95,97%. Na classificação de raios-X torácicos, o melhor resultado foi uma área sob a curva \nROC de 96,40%." ;
	:language "eng" ;
	:rights "openAccess" ;
	:createdBy :Ana_Filipa_de_Oliveira_Ramos ;
	:contributionBy :Victor_Alves, :Patrícia_Sánchez_Gonzalez ;
	:hasSubject :Deep_learning, :Machine_learning, :Medical_imaging, :MRI, :X_rays, :Imagens_ressonância_magnética, :Imagiologia_médica, :Raios_X ;
	:issuedDate "2019-11-08T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:availableDate "2022-10-06T17:38:39Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:accessionedDate "2022-10-06T17:38:39Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:submittedDate "2019-10-01T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:grade "18 valores" ;
	:rightsURI "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/" ;
	:hasOriginal :Ana_Filipa_de_Oliveira_Ramos_pdf ;
	:hasThumbnail :Ana_Filipa_de_Oliveira_Ramos_pdf_jpg .

:204227666 a :Record ;
	:title "Support system for managing curriculum vitae in LaTeX" ;
	:type "masterThesis" ;
	:description "This dissertation explores the domain of Curriculum Vitae (CV) management in an increasingly digital and\nweb-oriented world, with a particular focus on the challenges posed by LaTeX-based CVs. It examines the\nevolution of the CV as a fundamental document in both professional and academic contexts, highlighting\nthe limitations of existing solutions for efficiently managing and updating CVs across diverse templates.\nThe primary objective of this research is to design and implement a web-based system that simplifies\nthe management of LaTeX CV documents, providing flexibility and compatibility with a wide range of templates.\nA central aspect of this work is the investigation of Domain-Specific Languages, their foundational\nconcepts, and best practices, complemented by an analysis of widely used CV templates and established\nformatting standards within academic and professional communities.\nBy automating repetitive tasks and enhancing user efficiency, the proposed system aims to address\nthe deficiencies in current CV management solutions and deliver a robust, adaptable, and user-friendly\nplatform." ;
	:language "eng" ;
	:rights "openAccess" ;
	:createdBy :Júlio_Beites_Gonçalves ;
	:contributionBy :Pedro_Rangel_Henriques, :Maria_João_Varanda_Pereira ;
	:hasSubject :Curriculum_Vitae, :LaTeX, :Domain_Specific_Languages, :Linguagens_de_Domínio_Específico ;
	:issuedDate "2025-06-22T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:accessionedDate "2026-03-16T12:06:52Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:submittedDate "2025-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
	:grade "17 valores" ;
	:rightsURI "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/" ;
	:hasOriginal :Julio_Beites_Goncalves_pdf ;
	:hasThumbnail :Julio_Beites_Goncalves_pdf_jpg .

:203994264 a :Record ;
	:title "Automatic network design" ;
	:type "masterThesis" ;
	:description "With the increasing demand for high-quality internet services, deploying Gigabit Passive Optical Network/Fiberto-\nthe-Home networks is one of the biggest challenges internet providers have due to the significant investments\ninvolved. Automated network design usage becomes more critical to aid with planning the network\nby minimizing the costs of planning and deployment.\nIn today’s inventory environment, a designer typically takes around 1.5 days to create a network design\nfor a map with approximately 128 homes (a distribution cell). The challenges include: positioning the\ninstallation equipment such as cabinets, poles, or ducts; determining the number of apartments and\nhouses; planning cable pathways; defining the primary and secondary networks; establishing the influence\narea (which houses are served by each Optical Distribution Point); routing the cables; and setting up the\nconnections.\nThe design must be validated at the end to ensure it complies with the optical budget, coverage areas,\nsplitting, and necessary adjustments. The algorithms need to address several criteria to develop a feasible\nsolution. Specific rules and local-related variations, such as the maximum distance between the optical line\nterminal and the optical network unit or the permissible number of splits in a cable, must be considered,\nas well as preferences and constraints related to the network design.\nThis dissertation presents an AI-based solution that relies on a genetic algorithm using a two-level\nrepresentation to design the networks automatically. The business rationale behind implementing the\nsupported features (focused on the distribution cell) and the associated flexibility will be detailed, allowing\nfor easy adaptation and configuration according to specific rules. The validation of the approach will be\npresented by comparing the quality of the generated solutions with the handmade designs deployed in\nreal-world scenarios. The results will reassure that this method can save costs and time in finding suitable\nand better solutions than existing ones, indicating its potential as a support design tool of solutions for\nGigabit Passive Optical Network/Fiber-to-the-Home networks.\nOn average, across the five provided networks, it was possible to achieve a cost reduction of approximately\n50% and reduce the creation time from several hours to less than 30 seconds for all generated networks. Future work has already been outlined, including extending the automatic design to the primary\nnetwork and integrating the system into the inventory system." ;
	:language "eng" ;
	:rights "openAccess" ;
	:createdBy :João_Silva_Torres ;
	:contributionBy :Cesar_Analide ;
	:hasSubject :Fiber_to_the_Home, :Gigabit_Passive_Optical_Networks, :Graph_Theory, :Optimization, :Metaheuristic_Algorithms, :Genetic_Algorithms, :Steinner_Tree, :Teoria_dos_Grafos, :Otimização, :Algoritmos_Metaheurísticos, :Algoritmo_Genético, :Árvore_de_Steiner ;
